              <p>On a 1920s stone colonial in Wallingford, the plaster ceiling dipped almost half an inch over twelve feet &mdash; invisible to the eye, but enough to throw off a straight run of crown if we'd measured it like a new-construction job. We scribed each piece to the actual ceiling line instead of trusting the tape measure, which added a day to the install. The homeowner never saw a gap. That extra day is the difference between crown that looks store-bought and crown that looks like it was always there.</p>
